00:05:18.065 --> 00:05:21.500
I remember Lars from
Metallica was like, you know,

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:23.620
he was really mad about it.

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:26.850
Napster hijacked our music without asking.

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:28.910
They never sought our permission.

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:32.360
Our catalog of music simply
became available for free

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.840
downloads on the Napster system.

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:36.420
The argument I hear a lot

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:38.120
that music should be free,

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:40.930
must then mean the musicians
should work for free.

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:43.683
Nobody else works for
free, Why should musicians?

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:49.900
Drummer Lars Ulrich and
his band mates saying,

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:51.210
Napster is illegal

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:52.940
and filed suit against its creator

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:55.170
for copyright infringement.

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.480
Most artists weren't really too thrilled

00:05:57.480 --> 00:05:58.830
with being on the other side

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:01.423
of the kind of the hip
hot new young thing.

00:06:02.814 --> 00:06:05.770
But Lars was frankly unafraid

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:07.540
to get out there.

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:09.680
If they want to steal Metallica's music

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:11.880
instead of hiding behind their computers

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:13.410
in their bedrooms and dorm rooms

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:14.860
and just go down to Tower records

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:17.350
and grabbed them off the shelves instead.

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:21.640
When I saw Lars Ulrich
going on that campaign,

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:23.330
I just remember thinking
that was the lamest thing

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:24.233
I'd ever seen.

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:27.540
Like Metallica, you're all millionaires.

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:29.933
So, what are you complaining about?

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:36.730
What's your beef with Metallica then?

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:39.093
Why not let the people listen to music?

00:06:40.724 --> 00:06:42.970
As far as I'm concerned, Metallica

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:46.840
is off

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:49.083
my best album.

00:06:59.002 --> 00:07:02.690
There was definitely a lot of
hate and a lot of backlash.

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:04.050
My friend definitely said,

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:05.800
yeah, F Metallica, I'm done.

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:07.480
I'm only listening to Nirvana now.

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:09.940
I've removed all the
Metallica files from my,

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:11.250
from my hard drive,

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:13.730
I don't want anything to
do with Metallica anymore.

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:16.180
This mattered to a lot of people.

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:19.173
Napster was like this symbol of freedom.

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:22.840
And so when you have
guys like Lars Ulrich,

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:24.540
trying to shut Napster down,

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:26.640
you're going to have a hell of a backlash.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:31.590
The largest argument was
the one that I supported

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:35.050
and others in the industry did as well.

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:38.000
But this was an incredibly
disruptive force

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:38.870
in the music industries.

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:41.603
No matter what side of the
argument you came down on.

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:46.433
And the industry had to
shut all of this down.

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:03.717
Using a PC to download music

00:08:03.717 --> 00:08:06.640
is one of the hottest of
today's computer trends.

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:08.320
And that has the recording
companies up in arms

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:09.890
and heading to court.

00:08:09.890 --> 00:08:11.060
At the center of their dispute,

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:13.763
a music sharing internet
service known as Napster.

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:24.903
The litigation against
Napster was fairly monumental.

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:28.750
They were fighting us
in court, aggressively.

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:32.470
Napster's point of view is like,

00:08:32.470 --> 00:08:35.490
we don't personally share any files, Okay?

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:38.580
We just facilitate
connections between users

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:40.148
and we're not responsible for policing

00:08:40.148 --> 00:08:41.683
the contents of our network.

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:44.603
Pure and simple.

00:08:44.603 --> 00:08:46.510
It was theft of intellectual property.

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:50.230
And I just can't get past
that, that fundamental reality.

00:08:50.230 --> 00:08:51.520
I still get agitated

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:53.973
even at this point years later.

00:08:56.007 --> 00:08:57.220
Gentlemen, gentlemen.

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:01.359
CNN covered the court decision live.

00:09:01.359 --> 00:09:03.440
So there was a lot of pressure.

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:06.840
You know, 25 million
people were using Napster

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:09.663
every single day and really
invested in the outcome.

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:19.820
In the end, the judge ruled that,

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:21.530
it was obvious to everyone at Napster

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:23.363
that they knew what was going on.

00:09:24.430 --> 00:09:26.483
Napster appealed that and lost again.

00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:28.900
In a 68 page opinion,

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:30.200
the appeals court said,

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:32.480
Napster knew its users were violating

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:34.490
copyright infringement laws.

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:36.816
To song swapping service this weekend

00:09:36.816 --> 00:09:38.910
will begin blocking access

00:09:38.910 --> 00:09:41.563
to millions of musical files on Napster.

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:46.310
We stopped the sharing.

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:48.210
We basically took the servers offline.

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:52.370
Over a period of time the obligations

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:55.010
to the creditors just
overwhelmed the company

00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:58.100
and there didn't seem to be
any way to get revenue in

00:09:58.100 --> 00:10:00.210
and so the board determined at that point,

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:02.810
the best thing to do would
be to go into bankruptcy.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:07.103
The days of free music with
Napster folks are over.

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:11.740
It felt like it was
really dark horrible day

00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:12.813
in San Francisco.

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:16.743
I was depressed, lots of
people were depressed.

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:19.483
Technology had lost.

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:29.760
But then of course,

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:32.007
the mole started popping
all over the place.

00:10:38.380 --> 00:10:40.700
There are already dozens of alternatives

00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:41.560
to Napster,

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:43.840
with names such as My Napster,

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:44.673
Napigator,

00:10:44.673 --> 00:10:46.520
and Cute MX.

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:49.680
We had other file sharing services crop up

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:52.463
that were in many ways more insidious.

00:10:53.620 --> 00:10:55.950
There was no centralized server

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:58.230
that the files passed through.

00:10:58.230 --> 00:11:01.620
And that was much more
difficult to address.

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:04.440
Even if they cracked down
on one program like Kazaa

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:05.273
or something,

00:11:05.273 --> 00:11:07.360
I think there's going
to be another program

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:08.352
coming out in the next week or two,

00:11:08.352 --> 00:11:10.393
that's going to be doing the same thing.

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:16.140
The huge, huge problem
at the time is that,

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:18.230
it was very hard to tell people not to use

00:11:18.230 --> 00:11:20.630
peer-to-peer methods and they'll say,

00:11:20.630 --> 00:11:21.980
well, what should we use?

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:24.850
And the answer was, go to a
record store and buy a CD.

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:26.650
That's not what they wanted to hear.

00:11:27.550 --> 00:11:29.240
The record companies have to wake up

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:30.340
and I think they will,

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:32.150
to the fact that they're
no longer in the business

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:34.760
of hiring trucks and having big warehouses

00:11:34.760 --> 00:11:36.990
and shipping CDs around.

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:39.040
People want their music via the internet.

00:11:40.780 --> 00:11:43.571
The answer to the piracy
was to give people

00:11:43.571 --> 00:11:45.940
what they otherwise wanted,
in a legitimate way,

00:11:45.940 --> 00:11:48.940
at a fair and reasonable price.

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:53.223
We needed to have a
legitimate online music store.

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:01.490
The labels tried in their own lame ways

00:12:01.490 --> 00:12:03.800
to debut digital music
stores of their own,

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:05.233
which were total failures.

00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:09.680
I remember trying to use these things

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:11.200
and on these special players

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:12.120
and it was just,

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:13.900
it was really pretty antediluvian

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:17.093
and nobody really signed
up for these things.

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:19.643
For the longest time,

00:12:19.643 --> 00:12:21.730
there is such a divide
between Silicon Valley

00:12:21.730 --> 00:12:22.913
and the music industry.

00:12:24.399 --> 00:12:25.999
You know, it was just a big war.

00:12:28.910 --> 00:12:32.010
It was not in the software
industry's interest

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:33.360
to help the music industry.

00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:35.620
They sold a lot of computers

00:12:35.620 --> 00:12:38.140
because people wanted to get their music

00:12:38.140 --> 00:12:39.153
and other content for free.

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:43.627
The music companies, they said, you know,

00:12:43.627 --> 00:12:45.240
you guys are all promoting piracy

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:46.240
and the tech guys are like, Oh,

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:47.370
you guys are a bunch of Luddites,

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:50.560
you don't understand you have
to change your business model.

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:54.210
And the one guy who was
able to bridge that divide,

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:55.373
was Steve Jobs.

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:09.573
I've been back about,

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:11.620
eight to 10 weeks

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:14.931
and we've been working really hard.

00:13:14.931 --> 00:13:17.853
And what we're trying to do,

00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:21.070
is not something really highfalutin.

00:13:21.070 --> 00:13:22.550
We're trying to get back to the basics.

00:13:22.550 --> 00:13:25.320
Steve Jobs had been fired at Apple

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:27.390
and then been brought back.

00:13:27.390 --> 00:13:28.810
And at the time, he,

00:13:28.810 --> 00:13:31.500
Apple was in some ways,

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:34.503
a bit player in the computer space.

00:13:35.430 --> 00:13:36.890
The Mac products,

00:13:36.890 --> 00:13:40.112
represented about 3% market share.

00:13:40.112 --> 00:13:42.020
This company is absolutely
going to turn around.

00:13:42.020 --> 00:13:43.200
As a matter of fact,

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:44.069
I think the question now

00:13:44.069 --> 00:13:47.890
is not, can we turn around Apple?

00:13:47.890 --> 00:13:49.350
I think that's the booby prize.

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:50.183
I think it's,

00:13:50.183 --> 00:13:52.453
can we make Apple really great again?

00:13:56.500 --> 00:13:58.360
In 2001,

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:00.340
he introduced the iPod.

00:14:00.340 --> 00:14:01.690
There it is, right there.

00:14:05.172 --> 00:14:06.380
So, Steve saw a great opportunity

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:08.583
in the music space for Apple.

00:14:10.410 --> 00:14:12.760
Music has been around for a long time.

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:15.090
You know, it's a real tangible market.

00:14:15.090 --> 00:14:19.200
And, I think if we can bring
some value to that market,

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:20.150
then we'll succeed.

00:14:23.050 --> 00:14:24.920
In late 2002,

00:14:24.920 --> 00:14:26.440
Steve Jobs called and said

00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:29.910
that he was going to create
an online music store.

00:14:29.910 --> 00:14:32.780
And I saw that as the perfect
opportunity to at least say,

00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:34.830
all right, we've got something out there.

00:14:37.460 --> 00:14:38.710
We came up to Cupertino

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:41.740
and went into a conference room

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:43.523
and Steve walks in.

00:14:43.523 --> 00:14:46.720
I don't think we even knew
that that was going to happen.

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:48.310
But he walks in and says,

00:14:48.310 --> 00:14:49.880
I want to show you the iTunes store.

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:51.940
And then he sits there for two,

00:14:51.940 --> 00:14:53.930
himself with the mouse,

00:14:53.930 --> 00:14:55.743
showing how the store would work.

00:14:57.050 --> 00:14:59.210
He was like a little kid in a candy store.

00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:00.840
Absolutely, you know,

00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:03.600
fascinated with this thing
that I remember thinking,

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:04.840
it's so simple.

00:15:04.840 --> 00:15:07.993
And in fact it was, but
that's what made it brilliant.

00:15:08.930 --> 00:15:11.470
It worked better than anything
we'd ever seen before.

00:15:11.470 --> 00:15:13.123
And it became obvious this,

00:15:14.015 --> 00:15:15.433
this was a good thing to go with.

00:15:20.950 --> 00:15:23.990
The one thing of course
about the iTunes music store,

00:15:23.990 --> 00:15:26.723
he wanted every song to be a buck.

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:29.780
Steve Jobs wanted to sell singles,

00:15:29.780 --> 00:15:32.910
off of every album that was on iTunes.

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:35.570
He did not want a single
album on the store

00:15:35.570 --> 00:15:37.410
that you could not breakdown in singles.

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:41.030
That had, you know, really
serious ramifications.

00:15:41.030 --> 00:15:43.200
You know, for the music industry,

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:45.053
there would be unbundling the album.

00:15:53.570 --> 00:15:55.173
A CD is a bundle.

00:15:56.140 --> 00:15:57.340
It's a bundle of songs,

00:15:57.340 --> 00:16:00.243
if you want one song,
you the entire package.

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:04.290
Bundles inflate the total revenue

00:16:04.290 --> 00:16:06.790
because people are kind
of forced to pay up a bit.

00:16:08.060 --> 00:16:10.000
So let's take a canonical example,

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:11.387
which would be Vanilla Ice.

00:16:14.440 --> 00:16:16.190
I would be surprised if most people

00:16:17.157 --> 00:16:19.410
can't name more than one Vanilla Ice song.

00:16:19.410 --> 00:16:22.690
But Vanilla Ice sold something
like 10 million albums

00:16:22.690 --> 00:16:25.380
on the basis of just one hit song.

00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:29.093
So 150, $160 million in revenue.

00:16:30.910 --> 00:16:33.440
Once you liberated that
single from the album,

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:35.890
now excess income from the compact disc

00:16:36.801 --> 00:16:38.070
would go away forever.

00:16:38.070 --> 00:16:40.000
You would only earn a small fraction of

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:41.593
what you previously earned.

00:16:44.110 --> 00:16:46.010
They knew this was going to hurt them.

00:16:47.300 --> 00:16:49.797
It's , say it as a music fan,

00:16:49.797 --> 00:16:52.186
but it seems like music
is almost disposable now.

00:16:53.930 --> 00:16:56.380
You would put out an album,
what, every six years,

00:16:56.380 --> 00:16:59.640
tour for those six years,
off of that one album,

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:02.530
but now having to put out a
single and then three singles

00:17:02.530 --> 00:17:04.539
and then all of a sudden,

00:17:04.539 --> 00:17:05.747
it's just a string of singles

00:17:05.747 --> 00:17:07.340
with no cohesion like an album.

00:17:07.340 --> 00:17:09.790
And, really the songs
don't have as much meaning

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:11.240
to you as they did back then.

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:17.540
The pitch that Jobs
made to the majors was,

00:17:17.540 --> 00:17:18.863
we will save you.

00:17:20.342 --> 00:17:21.592
It was like a siren song.

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:24.880
There was no choice.

00:17:24.880 --> 00:17:26.060
Really, about anyone
who's thought about it,

00:17:26.060 --> 00:17:27.283
there was no choice.

00:17:28.750 --> 00:17:32.080
Today, we've got 200,000 tracks

00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:35.760
and all of this music
with all of these rights,

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:37.200
you can buy,

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:39.093
for 99 cents per song.

00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:51.090
It's called, iTunes.

00:17:51.090 --> 00:17:52.450
iTunes online music store.

00:17:52.450 --> 00:17:54.260
The iTunes music store.

00:17:54.260 --> 00:17:55.790
They've really just created, you know,

00:17:55.790 --> 00:17:57.050
what we've all been waiting for,

00:17:57.050 --> 00:17:59.910
which is a purely digital
music marketplace.

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:02.570
iTunes did spectacularly well.

00:18:02.570 --> 00:18:06.320
You had that great growth of downloads.

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:10.140
iTunes, recently sold
its 50 millionth song.

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:13.160
But the deal the industry made with Apple

00:18:13.160 --> 00:18:15.630
to me was a huge mistake.

00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:18.220
The unbundling of albums meant,

00:18:18.220 --> 00:18:22.803
that the revenue that came in
was significantly diminished.

00:18:23.810 --> 00:18:25.280
We were hopeful that the physical product

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:26.780
was hold on longer than that.

00:18:27.650 --> 00:18:29.630
As it turned out, it didn't.

00:18:29.630 --> 00:18:32.980
CD sales, have dropped almost one quarter

00:18:32.980 --> 00:18:34.160
in just three years.

00:18:34.160 --> 00:18:36.560
That's an awful lot of lost business.

00:18:36.560 --> 00:18:39.980
Buyers got very fed up with spending $18

00:18:39.980 --> 00:18:42.410
when there's only one
or two songs they like.

00:18:42.410 --> 00:18:45.070
This change with iTunes
made people so upset

00:18:45.070 --> 00:18:46.450
that people actually start taking

00:18:46.450 --> 00:18:48.570
the products off the platform.

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:51.600
Estelle had an album that had come out

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:54.930
and the label found out that
about 90% of the purchases

00:18:54.930 --> 00:18:57.563
were just a one song, American boy.

00:18:58.880 --> 00:19:02.210
They pulled the entire album
off of platform for three weeks

00:19:02.210 --> 00:19:04.593
before caving in and putting it back on.

00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:12.660
Really, they were desperate.

00:19:12.660 --> 00:19:14.500
And they decided what they would do,

00:19:14.500 --> 00:19:18.273
was go after the end users
of the peer-to-peer sites.

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:21.190
The average file sharer,

00:19:21.190 --> 00:19:23.560
John and Jane download.

00:19:23.560 --> 00:19:26.430
This was extremely controversial
within the record industry.

00:19:26.430 --> 00:19:28.740
Hilary Rosen, the head of the RIAA,

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:30.880
actually resigned in protest.

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:33.830
I didn't want us to go
against individuals,

00:19:33.830 --> 00:19:36.310
even though they were the source of

00:19:36.310 --> 00:19:38.540
a huge amount of illegal activity,

00:19:38.540 --> 00:19:42.330
I felt like ultimately
they were still music fans.

00:19:42.330 --> 00:19:44.483
But essentially I was kind of overruled.

00:19:46.940 --> 00:19:49.610
My last day at the RIAA was the day before

00:19:49.610 --> 00:19:52.513
the litigation against
individuals started.

00:20:00.160 --> 00:20:02.860
Yesterday, the music
industry took another step

00:20:02.860 --> 00:20:03.693
for the first time,

00:20:03.693 --> 00:20:07.490
going after people downloading
and swapping music online.

00:20:07.490 --> 00:20:11.320
We want people to stop
engaging in the theft of music.

00:20:11.320 --> 00:20:13.853
So that people can go on making it.

00:20:19.510 --> 00:20:22.750
I don't remember if I got
it, via letter or email.

00:20:22.750 --> 00:20:26.100
But I basically got
what looked like a scam

00:20:26.100 --> 00:20:29.230
from the RIAA saying,

00:20:29.230 --> 00:20:31.563
you owe us for having downloaded music.

00:20:32.670 --> 00:20:37.670
I honestly ignored it, cause
I didn't care and I got,

00:20:37.750 --> 00:20:40.360
they sent another letter saying,

00:20:40.360 --> 00:20:44.257
I could be sued for up
to $30,000 per song,

00:20:44.257 --> 00:20:46.333
of which there were thousands.

00:20:48.830 --> 00:20:50.453
I didn't have that much money.

00:20:52.230 --> 00:20:54.050
I don't think anyone did.

00:20:54.050 --> 00:20:56.930
I wasn't ever really
worried about getting fined

00:20:56.930 --> 00:20:58.700
for downloading MP3s mainly because

00:20:58.700 --> 00:21:00.600
I was using my mom's computer

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:02.450
and I was kind of an ,

00:21:02.450 --> 00:21:05.120
so I was like, Oh my mom's
going to get fined, not me.

00:21:05.120 --> 00:21:07.900
Like, you can't take a
13 year old's income,

00:21:07.900 --> 00:21:09.603
when he doesn't have any income.

00:21:13.150 --> 00:21:16.192
They filed something like 17,000 lawsuits.

00:21:19.010 --> 00:21:21.490
Rather than going after the big infringer,

00:21:21.490 --> 00:21:24.420
they ended up suing
children and grandmothers

00:21:24.420 --> 00:21:25.720
and people who were dead

00:21:25.720 --> 00:21:27.980
and you know, moms.

00:21:27.980 --> 00:21:29.350
Bonnie Bassett was shocked

00:21:29.350 --> 00:21:32.250
to be slapped with a lawsuit
for the hundreds of songs,

00:21:32.250 --> 00:21:35.370
her teens downloaded off
the internet for free.

00:21:35.370 --> 00:21:37.850
My kids go to Catholic school, we pray,

00:21:37.850 --> 00:21:38.683
we brought them up right,

00:21:38.683 --> 00:21:40.500
no one in our family has
been arrested, I mean,

00:21:40.500 --> 00:21:42.050
we don't steal, We don't steal.

00:21:42.990 --> 00:21:44.440
It's just rid...,

00:21:44.440 --> 00:21:46.400
It was absurd. It was absurd.

00:21:46.400 --> 00:21:49.120
I'm not downloading anything ever again.

00:21:49.120 --> 00:21:51.743
I mean, not even screensavers.

00:21:53.290 --> 00:21:55.600
Almost everybody was forced to settle,

00:21:55.600 --> 00:21:57.750
often for a relatively
small amount of money,

00:21:57.750 --> 00:21:59.283
like three or $4,000.

00:22:00.530 --> 00:22:02.933
But in the end, this just
made people hate them.

00:22:03.770 --> 00:22:05.510
They don't create a deterrent effect,

00:22:05.510 --> 00:22:07.950
it just made them loathed.

00:22:07.950 --> 00:22:09.900
You know, I've been an
avid record collector

00:22:09.900 --> 00:22:11.216
for over 40 years.

00:22:11.216 --> 00:22:12.049
Right.

00:22:12.049 --> 00:22:13.433
I've got nearly 10,000 records.

00:22:13.433 --> 00:22:16.510
And you turned a music fan

00:22:16.510 --> 00:22:17.463
into an enemy.

00:22:18.642 --> 00:22:20.550
In fact, more people
probably went to piracy

00:22:20.550 --> 00:22:22.603
as a result of this than anything else.

00:22:25.230 --> 00:22:27.810
I ended up having to pay like,

00:22:27.810 --> 00:22:31.850
I think close to a thousand
dollars to the RIAA.

00:22:31.850 --> 00:22:34.070
But in the end I ended
up recouping my losses

00:22:34.070 --> 00:22:37.773
by downloading overtime and doubling down.

00:22:49.950 --> 00:22:53.230
Between the piracy and the unbundling,

00:22:53.230 --> 00:22:56.543
the industry recognized
that it was on a decline.

00:22:58.640 --> 00:23:01.650
And over the course of, you know,

00:23:01.650 --> 00:23:03.340
15 years,

00:23:03.340 --> 00:23:05.853
shrank significantly.

00:23:09.210 --> 00:23:10.043
Industry revenue,

00:23:10.043 --> 00:23:12.970
I think in 99 was worldwide 34 billion

00:23:14.300 --> 00:23:17.123
and it fell to something like 16 billion.

00:23:19.170 --> 00:23:22.513
The industry was just
destroyed, annihilated.

00:23:26.785 --> 00:23:29.300
A simple dynamic in business,

00:23:29.300 --> 00:23:31.560
when an industry is under threat,

00:23:31.560 --> 00:23:32.733
you consolidate.

00:23:33.810 --> 00:23:34.860
which is a

00:23:34.860 --> 00:23:37.033
fancier word for cutting costs.

00:23:38.690 --> 00:23:41.010
You put two big music
enterprises together,

00:23:41.010 --> 00:23:43.010
you can eliminate a whole bunch of Jobs.

00:23:58.200 --> 00:24:00.340
I'm currently standing in
front of what used to be the

00:24:00.340 --> 00:24:02.063
Virgin Mega store union square,

00:24:03.060 --> 00:24:04.920
closed in June of 2009.

00:24:04.920 --> 00:24:07.873
And is now Duane Reade and a Citibank.

00:24:13.420 --> 00:24:16.003
This building formerly
used to be tower records.

00:24:17.830 --> 00:24:20.843
It was the first tower
records in New York.

00:24:21.880 --> 00:24:23.960
That window right there
where the light is,

00:24:23.960 --> 00:24:25.850
that floor I worked on,

00:24:25.850 --> 00:24:27.080
was the,

00:24:27.080 --> 00:24:28.163
oldie section.

00:24:30.050 --> 00:24:32.070
CD sales were dipping

00:24:32.070 --> 00:24:34.800
since I think like 2000, 2001.

00:24:34.800 --> 00:24:37.120
That's where the decline really kicked in.

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:39.470
I can't even remember the
last time I physically went

00:24:39.470 --> 00:24:41.040
and bought a CD.

00:24:41.040 --> 00:24:42.770
What 2006 happened,

00:24:42.770 --> 00:24:45.370
was all over for me.

00:24:45.370 --> 00:24:47.040
That was the end of it.

00:24:47.040 --> 00:24:49.150
It was much I could do.

00:24:49.150 --> 00:24:51.310
Tower records may be toppling,

00:24:51.310 --> 00:24:52.330
the music chain is preparing

00:24:52.330 --> 00:24:54.790
to file for bankruptcy next week.

00:24:54.790 --> 00:24:56.450
When tower was bought out,

00:24:56.450 --> 00:24:57.540
a lot of us,

00:24:57.540 --> 00:24:58.880
the employee that worked there,

00:24:58.880 --> 00:25:01.460
we fell into like a depression.

00:25:01.460 --> 00:25:03.296
We were like, wow, this, this is it.

00:25:03.296 --> 00:25:06.760
Tower is going to be gone for good.

00:25:06.760 --> 00:25:08.033
How do I feel about it?

00:25:09.390 --> 00:25:10.223
Crappy.

